; A commodity already written with a decimal period may still meet a
; comma that only its digits call decimal, without error, where the
; amount does not teach the commodity its style.  A format sub-directive
; or a D directive declares the style, so "1000,00" there switches the
; commodity to the decimal comma, as it always has.  A cost (and a P
; price) never teaches the style, so "$0,075" there is read as 0.075
; locally.  Only an amount that teaches the commodity its style is an
; error in that case: see decimal-comma-conflict.test.

2024/01/01 EUR written with a decimal period
    A:EUR    1.50 EUR
    B

commodity EUR
    format 1000,00 EUR

2024/01/02 EUR after its declared format
    A:EUR    2,25 EUR
    B

2024/01/03 SEK written with a decimal period
    A:SEK    1.50 SEK
    B

D 1000,00 SEK

2024/01/04 SEK after its D directive
    A:SEK    2,25 SEK
    B

test bal --flat --no-total ^A:
            3,75 EUR  A:EUR
            3,75 SEK  A:SEK
end test

2024/01/05 Dollars written with a decimal period
    C:USD    $1,000.50
    D

2024/01/06 A cost whose comma only a guess reads
    C:XAU    10 XAU @ $0,075
    D

test bal -B --flat --no-total ^C:
           $1,000.50  C:USD
               $0.75  C:XAU
end test
